    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you expand a bit more on why we shouldn't just go with vertical scaling every time? Like, what are the specific risks or limitations when a sudden spike hits and we're just relying on more powerful single instances? I want to make sure they understand why horizontal is the \"safer\" bet for our architecture.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Relying solely on vertical scaling introduces a significant limitation known as a ceiling; eventually, there's a point where you simply cannot make a single instance powerful enough. Furthermore, if that single, powerful instance fails due to hardware or configuration issues, the entire service goes down completely. Horizontal scaling mitigates this single point of failure by distributing the load, meaning one instance's failure will only degrade performance slightly, not crash the whole system. This inherent redundancy makes horizontal scaling the safer, more resilient architecture for handling sudden, unpredictable demand.",
      "source": "target",
